A||N||14||SloPonti||questions on lowering the fiero!!!||||7||
Z||000000||SloPonti||10-13-2000||12:46 AM||||i have an 84 fiero with 16 inch z24 wheels! i plan to drop the fiero this winter! i have been doing research using the big fiero parts book and the lowering springs range any were from 3/4 of an inch to 2 inch drops! what is the best kit for the money! 2 inches would really fill up my fender well gap but i also have neon lights under the car and do not want to be replacing them! i also auto cross the car quite a bit and i am also considering performance to! i have replaced all suspension and done all poly bushing to! see picture of car at- <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/my55ride/z24.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/my55ride/z24.jpg</A>  <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/my55ride/z242.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/my55ride/z242.jpg</A> <p>[This message has been edited by SloPonti (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||10-13-2000||03:23 AM||||I have the Eibach Pro-Kit Lowering Springs. Ive havent bottomed out yet. The only problem is that you have to be carful when pulling into Oil Change places & Automatic Car Washes.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/a_1.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif">  <BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A>-                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>||||reg||14
Z||000002||william_thorniii||10-13-2000||08:11 AM||||Slo,<P>If you want the best auto-x performance perhaps you should consider spindles.  They are a bit more expensive but they keep the geometry correct and allow you to lower a vehicle without any detrimental effects that sometimes accompany it.  <P>Haze,<P>What is your take on your springs?  I have heard personally from those that hate them and from those that love them.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000003||Oreif||10-13-2000||09:43 AM||||I have the Eibach springs on my car. The car lowered about 1 1/4". I am using poly bushings, KYB shocks and struts. For tires I am running 225/50R15's all the way around. The car handles great. It sticks to the road like glue. The ride is VERY stiff. If you want to keep your ride quality dropped spindles would be the way to go. I prefer a stiff ride. Also adding an aftermarket rear sway bar will help cornering without sacraficing ride.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1986SE.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <P>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-13-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||goldfinger||10-13-2000||02:18 PM||||Where can I buy eibach springs to drop my 86 2m4? Also are they hard to put on?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000005||Oreif||10-13-2000||02:26 PM||||I bought mine from the Fiero Store. I know they are available thru other places.<BR>Swapping springs is easy if you just replace the springs. I did the entire suspension at once. (Bushings, shocks, struts, ball joints, tie rods, sway bars, brakes, etc)||||reg||1
Z||000006||the need for speed||10-13-2000||02:57 PM||||go to <A HREF="http://www.shox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com</A>  for the best prize<BR>eibach springs, i think was $219<BR>addco sway bar was $129<BR>koni's $449||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||10-13-2000||03:22 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.cyberauto.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cyberauto.com</A> <P>and <BR> <A HREF="http://www.carparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.carparts.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||10-13-2000||04:12 PM||||For '88 owners interested, I have been told that the Fiero Store springs are better then the Eibach springs.  I know someone who is going to switch them on his '88 GT.  <P><BR>Just passing along the word...<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||12
Z||000009||SloPonti||10-13-2000||05:58 PM||||hey haze what was the max drop you got out of your lowering springs! orief said he got 1 1/4-are you the same! also were did you order from haze?<BR>thanks guys!!!!<BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000010||Haze_Performance||10-13-2000||06:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SloPonti:<BR><B>hey haze what was the max drop you got out of your lowering springs! orief said he got 1 1/4-are you the same! also were did you order from haze?<BR>thanks guys!!!!<BR> Rob</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got mine from The Fiero Store. Oreif's car looks lower than mine.... but I think its because he has a larger tire (His tires fill up the wheel wells more). Mine are 205/40ZR17. + he has the SE ground EFX.. That maks his car look even lower.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif">  <BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A>-                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>||||reg||14
Z||000011||BAM-BAM||10-13-2000||07:23 PM|||| SLOPONTI, I have the same setup on my 85GT that Oerif has (except wheels and tires) I have about an 1"-1 1/4" drop using the Eibachs. The ride is "very" stiff, and I like the control and response. I think when all is considered the Eibach's with the KYB's is the most cost effective. You could go with the Koni's if your budget permits, that would give you greater ride control for your changing needs, ie...Auto-x, daily driving, ect.. The springs are not difficult to change out at all. But, while you are there you might as well change what ever is left. Others have cut there springs and swear by it, so there are lots of optiond for you. Good luck and let us know how it turns out.<P>  Later, BAM OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach Springs/KYB shocks and struts<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats, Carpet and headliner! YEAH BABY-YEAH<p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||2xsess||10-13-2000||08:02 PM||||I'm thinking of buying the lowering springs from carparts.com, has anyone used them?<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||10-14-2000||12:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Haze_Performance:<BR><B> I got mine from The Fiero Store. Oreif's car looks lower than mine.... but I think its because he has a larger tire (His tires fill up the wheel wells more). Mine are 205/40ZR17. + he has the SE ground EFX.. That maks his car look even lower.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Haze is correct, his tires have a smaller overall diameter where mine are the same diameter as the original tires. So I "fill the wheel well" better. I bought the entire KYB kit from the Fiero Store and with the clubs 10% discount I paid around $650 with shipping.<BR>The kit comes with KYB shocks, KYB struts, Eibach springs, and both front/rear swaybars. The sway bars are bigger than stock, come with all poly bushings, and are made by KONI. If you price it out all the parts thru someplace else you'll be paying the same if not slightly more. (compared to the Fiero Store price with 10% discount). <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pontiaddict||10-15-2000||01:37 AM||||I just changed most of my suspension last week.<BR>The Ebiachs stiffen the ride, but you get used to it.<BR>I bought the KYB suspension kit from the Fiero Store. The rear bar is on backorder by the way.<BR>||||reg||1
